; Fallible - the effect a batch loop's deep callee performs on a
; malformed record, and the handlers that answer it without unwinding.
;
; WHAT THIS IS FOR. A batch job reads four million records and the
; 4,000,001st is malformed. The function that noticed is six calls
; below the loop, and what should happen next - skip the record, use a
; default, count it and carry on - is the LOOP's decision, not the
; parser's. `Result` (`Err.ax`) would carry the fault up six frames by
; hand, one `match` per frame, and then hand the loop a value it can
; only inspect after the frames are gone. An effect operation asks the
; question at the point it arises and continues with the answer:
;
;   (fallibleMalformed "quantity is not a number")
;
; dispatches to the innermost handler, and the handler's return value
; IS the operation's result (reference.md, Handling Effects). Nothing
; unwinds, nothing is retried, no frame between the loop and the
; parser knows anything happened. That is `ERR-REC-3`'s distinction
; exactly - `effect` is for capabilities, `Result` is for failure - and
; the capability here is "ask the loop what to do with this record".
; `docs/error-model.md` `ERR-REC-7` states the rule and the fixture
; that holds it (`tests/stdlib/410-fallible.ax`).
;
; ONE ARGUMENT, AND IT IS A LITERAL. Both were decided by measurement
; against the alternatives, on 2026-08-29, with the arena mark cell as
; the instrument (`370-error-propagation.ax`'s method: word 0 is the
; bump, word 2 the chunk) over 10,000 records of which 1,428 performed
; the operation, inside one chunk:
;
;   one argument, a literal message       0 bytes per operation
;   one argument, an `Int` index          0 bytes per operation
;   two arguments (message, fallback)    32 bytes per operation
;   one argument, a message BUILT         80 bytes per operation
;     per record (`strConcat`/`fmtInt`)
;
; TWO OF THOSE ROWS HAVE MOVED SINCE, and the reasons written under
; them were not both right. Re-measured 2026-08-30 on the same
; instrument:
;
;   two arguments (message, fallback)     0 bytes per operation
;   one argument, a message BUILT        80 bytes per operation
;
; The two-argument row was correct about its cause - a two-argument
; operation's handler is a curried chain, answering one allocates the
; inner closure, and the dispatch never released it - and that is now
; fixed: both chain walkers release the record a previous step
; returned once the next step has consumed it, so an intermediate is
; born at count 1 and files at 0 (`emitApplyRegsOwned`,
; `emitApplyChainOwned`; `scripts/check-closure-reclaim.sh`).
;
; The BUILT-MESSAGE row's stated cause is FALSE and is corrected here
; rather than left standing. It said "a handler parameter's type is a
; variable, so the release walk cannot classify it and nothing frees
; the string". Measured: a fully-typed NAMED function passed as a
; value leaks the same 96 bytes, and so does a lambda whose parameter
; is `String` - the type has nothing to do with it. What leaks is that
; an APPLICATION THROUGH A CLOSURE never releases its owned argument
; at all, where a direct call has released it since MM-LIFE-2g
; (`releaseOwnedArgs`); the handler's typing, which landed separately,
; moved no bytes. That half is not fixed yet, so the rule below still
; holds for messages and the row above still reads 80. The plan this
; module comes from says why either would be disqualifying - a batch
; loop has no request boundary, so there is no arena reset
; (`memory-model.md` MM-ALLOC-22) and every byte a record costs is a
; byte the process keeps. So the operation takes the message alone,
; the fallback value comes from the HANDLER (`fallibleDefault` is
; built once, at the `handle`), and the message is a literal that says
; what was wrong, not which record - the loop knows which record it is
; on. `scripts/check-steady-state.sh`'s `batch` probe holds the
; consequence: 2,000,000 records under `fallibleSkip` at the same RSS
; as 200,000.
;
; "SKIP" IS A SENTINEL THE LOOP CHECKS. The operation answers an
; `Int`, so a handler that means "skip this one" has to answer
; SOMETHING; `fallibleSkipped` is that value and `fallibleIsSkipped` is
; how a loop reads it. It is the most negative `Int` - a value no
; record quantity takes, no heap address is, and `Err.intMin` spells
; the same way - rather than an `Option`, because `(Some v)` on every
; WELL-FORMED record is a block per record, which is the cost this
; module exists to avoid. A program whose records really can hold that
; value writes its own handler answering its own sentinel; a handler is
; a one-line lambda.
;
; THE HANDLERS COMPOSE. `fallibleCounting` counts and delegates to the
; handler it was given, so "count them, and skip" and "count them, and
; use 0" are both one expression. It delegates by calling the closure
; it holds rather than by performing `fallibleMalformed` again - a
; handler that re-performs its own effect dispatches OUTWARD to the
; next handler, which is correct and traps when there is none, and a
; counting handler that only works inside another handler is a trap
; waiting for the first program to install it alone.
;
; WHAT AN UNHANDLED OPERATION DOES. Exits 71, `axiom: unhandled effect`
; on fd 2 - `ERR-REC-6`'s class (ii), a programmer error and not a
; record's fault - and inside a recovery point it answers 71 to the
; arming call. The fixture pins both. This is also why the effect is
; declared without `pub`: an `effect` is exported unconditionally
; (reference.md, Modules), so the operation is callable from any file
; that imports `Fallible`, with or without a name list.
;
; The effect. One operation, because the compiler handles
; single-operation effects dynamically and one custom effect per
; `handle` (reference.md, Handling Effects); a program wanting two
; kinds of question declares two effects and nests the handles.
(effect Fallible
  (fallibleMalformed :: (-> String Int)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The skip protocol
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The value a handler answers to mean "skip this record": the most
; negative `Int`. A loop compares the value it got against this, or
; asks `fallibleIsSkipped`.
(pub :: fallibleSkipped Int)

(pub fn (fallibleSkipped) (- (- 0 9223372036854775807) 1))

; Whether a value is the skip sentinel. The comparison a batch loop
; makes once per record; it allocates nothing.
(pub :: fallibleIsSkipped (-> Int Bool))

(pub fn (fallibleIsSkipped v) (== v fallibleSkipped))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The ready handlers. Each is a value a program passes to `handle`:
;
;   (handle (processAll records) (Fallible) fallibleSkip)
;   (handle (processAll records) (Fallible) (fallibleDefault 0))
;   (handle (processAll records) (Fallible Mut)
;     (fallibleCounting tally fallibleSkip))
;
; A `handle` expression's type is its body's, and each handler below
; is checked against `fallibleMalformed`'s arrow, `(-> String Int)`:
; `(println (handle ...))` renders the `Int` with no cast, a lambda
; handler's `msg` IS a `String`, and a handler of another shape is
; AX3004 at the `handle`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Skip every malformed record: answer `fallibleSkipped`, whatever the
; message. A one-parameter top-level function is a value, so it is
; passed bare.
(pub :: fallibleSkip (-> String Int))

(pub fn (fallibleSkip msg) fallibleSkipped)

; Use `d` in place of every malformed record. Built ONCE, at the
; `handle`, which is why the fallback is here and not an argument of
; the operation: the closure holding `d` is allocated when the handler
; is installed, not when a record is bad. One parameter, answering the
; handler - the type is spelled flat because every function type is
; curried and that is the formatter's normal form; `mkAdder` in
; `280-function-application.ax` is the precedent.
(pub :: fallibleDefault (-> Int String Int))

(pub fn (fallibleDefault d)
  (lambda (msg) d)
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Counting
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; How many records were malformed. A struct rather than a bare `Int`
; because the handler has to write it from inside a closure, and a
; field store is the one mutation visible through every holder of the
; value (reference.md, Built-in Effects: `Mut`).
(pub struct FallibleTally
  (count : Int))

; A fresh tally at zero.
(pub :: fallibleTally FallibleTally)

(pub fn (fallibleTally) (FallibleTally 0))

; What a tally holds.
(pub :: fallibleCount (-> FallibleTally Int))

(pub fn (fallibleCount t) t.count)

; Count every malformed record in `tally`, then answer as `next` would:
; `(fallibleCounting t fallibleSkip)` skips and counts,
; `(fallibleCounting t (fallibleDefault 0))` substitutes and counts.
; The `handle` installing it lists `Mut` beside `Fallible`, because a
; handler's own effects count at the site that installs it.
(pub :: fallibleCounting (-> FallibleTally (-> String Int) String Int))

(pub fn (fallibleCounting tally next)
  (lambda (msg)
    {
      (set tally.count (+ tally.count 1))
      (next msg)
    }
  )
)
